  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Continued dry with seasonable temperatures through \nthe middle of next week. \n\nSurface analysis shows broad 1030+mb high pressure centered near \nnorthern NY early this afternoon. Aloft, the flow is highly \namplified between strong ridging over the central CONUS and low \nheights over the Canadian Maritimes. A weakening closed upper low is \nnoted off the Carolina coast. A subtle surface trough has formed \nover the eastern half of the area as high pressure to the north \nspreads eastward. Low level winds have become onshore/NE, allowing \nmoisture to increase below a subsidence inversion aloft. Scattered \nto broken stratocumulus clouds have blossomed over the area this \nafternoon with the relative thickest clouds mostly confined to the \neastern half of the area. \n\nThe high continues to build south and into the local area later \ntonight through the weekend, with a developing sfc trough of low \npressure off the SE US coast. The compressed pressure gradient will \ntend to keep the airmass mixed with milder temperatures for at least \nthe SE 1/2 of the CWA tonight but northern and NW sections should \nsee lows back into the 30s, potentially leading to patchy frost by \nsunrise Saturday. Held off on any Frost Advisories after \ncoordination with neighboring offices, mainly due to the potential \nfor at least light winds to continue overnight. The typically cooler \nsheltered spots in the piedmont will have the best chance to see \nsome patchy frost early Saturday morning. \n\nHigh pressure will dominate the local weather pattern for the \nremainder of the weekend and likely well into next week. Not much \nchange in the forecast with seasonal temperatures and near zero \nchance for rainfall returning to the region. At this time, both\nSaturday night/Sunday AM and Sunday night/Monday AM have the \npotential for at least patchy frost over much of the interior, \nwith the greatest potential in the piedmont. Have continued to \nundercut NBM and some of the statistical guidance values by \nseveral degrees except at the immediate coast. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Elevated tidal anomalies continue through Saturday \nwith nuisance to minor tidal flooding forecast.\n\nThe remains of Josephine have pushed well away from the local area, \nbut with swell still propagating toward the coast and high pressure \nto the north, tidal anomalies remain 1.5-2ft above normal.\nThese anomalies should remain steady and/or slowly fall tonight into \nSaturday. Therefore, primarily nuisance to minor tidal flooding is \nexpected and Coastal Flood Advisories have been extended through the \nSaturday morning/early aftn high tide cycle for some locations, \nprimarily for the tidal Rappahannock, southern shore of the tidal \nPotomac, the upper York, and the middle and upper Ches. Bay. \nNuisance flooding may persist into Saturday night/Sunday as swell \ncontinues to propagate toward the coast with persistent (but weaker) \nonshore flow, as well as a gradual increase in astronomical high \ntides as we approach full moon.",
